 <section
    id="challenge"
    class="project-section w3-padding-64"
    aria-labelledby="challenge-heading">
        <h2 id="challenge-heading">The challenge</h2>

        <p class="w3-large">
            The only source material provided for this project was a one-hour
            recording of a panel discussion between a Master of Social Work
            student and four therapists working in the field. I was asked to turn
            that recording into a complete, two-hour, self-paced continuing
            education course.
        </p>
        <p>
            The panel contained relevant practitioner perspectives, but a
            recording alone did not provide the structure, scope, interaction, or
            supporting evidence required for a continuing education experience.
            The final course needed to be sufficiently detailed and applicable
            for practicing social workers while meeting a defined two-hour
            duration.
        </p>

        <p>
            I was not the subject-matter expert. I therefore needed to research
            the field, identify important content not addressed by the panel,
            select reliable external sources, and integrate that material without
            extending beyond the available evidence. A subject-matter expert
            reviewed and validated the completed course before launch.
        </p>

        <p>
            The course also addressed sensitive and potentially difficult
            material. The design needed to communicate that clearly, allow
            learners to control their progress, and present the content without
            removing the professional detail required by the intended audience.
        </p>
        <p>From a technical perspective, the course needed to function as a
            self-paced Storyline module, record completion through Blackboard,
            direct learners to a separate post-course quiz, retain progress when
            learners exited, and meet accessibility requirements.</p>




    </section>

    <!-- Solution -->
 <section
    id="solution"
    class="project-section w3-padding-32"
    aria-labelledby="solution-heading">
        <h2 id="solution-heading">The solution</h2>

        <p>
            I independently designed and developed a four-part continuing
            education course around the panel discussion. Rather than presenting
            the recording as a single video, I divided it into more than 20 short
            clips organized by topic. The clips range from approximately one to
            ten minutes, allowing each practitioner discussion to be paired with
            relevant research, instruction, and reflection.
        </p>

        <p>
            I wrote separate learning objectives for each part of the course and
            used those objectives to determine which panel excerpts to include,
            where additional instruction was required, and how learners would
            engage with the material.
        </p>

        <p>
            I conducted independent research to address topics that the panel did
            not cover in sufficient depth. Research and external media were embedded within the instructional
            sequence rather than added as a separate resource list. Learners move
            between practitioner perspectives, research findings, explanatory
            content, and guided reflection questions.
        </p>

        <p>
            I built the complete experience in Articulate Storyline and published
            it as a SCORM package. The course records module completion through
            Blackboard, where learners complete the required post-course quiz.
        </p>

    </section>

